Don't match a mount point by an unknown device number on Windows.
find_mount_point() picks the first drive or mounted folder whose
st_dev, the volume serial number, equals the file's. stat() gives a
device of 0 when it cannot read the serial number, for instance for a
file it cannot open or a cloud file that has not been downloaded, so
such a file matched the first mount point whose serial number also
could not be read, and "df FILE" and "stat -f FILE" silently reported
an unrelated volume. Don't match on a device of 0; a file named by its
drive or mount point still matches by name. Builds without
FEATURE_EXTRA_FILE_DATA, where every device is 0, are unchanged.

---
 libbb/find_mount_point.c |    7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/libbb/find_mount_point.c
+++ b/libbb/find_mount_point.c
@@ -94,8 +94,11 @@
 			break;
 		}
 
-		/* Match the directory's mount point. */
-		if (stat(mountEntry->mnt_dir, &s) == 0
+		/* Match the directory's mount point.  A device of 0 means
+		 * the volume serial number couldn't be read: it matches
+		 * nothing, unless device numbers aren't read at all. */
+		if ((devno_of_name != 0 || !ENABLE_FEATURE_EXTRA_FILE_DATA)
+		 && stat(mountEntry->mnt_dir, &s) == 0
 		 && s.st_dev == devno_of_name
 		) {
 			break;
